 178:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****     /* NOTE: In assigning values to A,B,C:
 179:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      *  the "sampling point" (which is A+B) has to be as far as possible
 180:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      *  from the "shifting point" (end of A+B+C).
 181:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      * The duty cycle is calculated as the ratio of A : B+C
 182:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      * Therefore, to keep 50% duty cycle: A = B+C
 183:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      * Thus, we cannot influence the value of A, once A+B+C is found out
 184:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      * (A+1 is always (A+B+C)/2).
 185:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      * However, in between B and C - we should try to maximize B (and minimize C).
 186:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      * The goal will be to do this - keep max value of B always and keep only any remaining value f
 187:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****      */
 188:../Libraries/iLLD/TC4xx/Tricore/Qspi/Std/IfxQspi.c ****     halfBaud     = bestAbc / 2;
